# Next.js (this repo)

Also see [Cursor Directory — Next.js plugin](https://cursor.directory/plugins/nextjs): same spirit—**avoid unnecessary duplication and complexity**, pick the right tool, security-aware.

## Before writing Next code

Read the guide in `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` for this installed version. **Do not assume training-data Next.js** — APIs differ (see root `AGENTS.md`).

## Async dynamic APIs (required)

In Server Components, layouts, pages, route handlers, and `generateMetadata`:

- `params` and `searchParams` are **`Promise`s** — `await` them.
- `cookies()` and `headers()` from `next/headers` — **`await`** before use.

```tsx
type Props = { params: Promise<{ slug: string }> };

export default async function Page({ params }: Props) {
  const { slug } = await params;
}
```

In **Client** components that receive a params Promise, use `React.use(params)`.

## App structure (Sairr)

- Routes: `src/app/` · UI: `src/components/` · shared logic: `src/lib/` (validations, `google-sheets`, etc.)
- Prefer **Route Handlers** in `src/app/api/` for mutations used by forms (e.g. `send-data-to-sheet`).
- **Server Actions** only if the user asks; treat as public POST endpoints—validate input, no secrets in client bundles.

## Security

- Never commit `.env` or service account keys.
- API routes: validate body (Zod), allowlist sheet IDs server-side, no trusting client-only checks.
- Forms: keep PII handling minimal; match existing patterns in `lib/validations/*`.

## Simplicity

Project rule `simplicity.mdc` wins over generic “best practice” that adds files or layers.
